{
  "id": "u02-l04",
  "unit": "u02-pronouns",
  "title": "Is, isn't & yes/no",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "To ask a yes/no question, add **কি** (*ki*), usually right after the subject: **আপনি কি শিক্ষক?** 'Are you a teacher?' Spelling: **কি** (short ি) is the question marker; **কী** (long ী) means 'what'. They sound the same: **এটা কী?** 'What is this?' vs **ওটা কি আম?** 'Is that a mango?'\n\n'Is' has no word in Bengali, but **'is not'** does — and it changes with the person:\n\n- আমি ছাত্র **নই** — I'm not a student\n- তুমি ছাত্র **নও** — you're not\n- আপনি / উনি ছাত্র **নন** — you / he (polite) aren't\n- সে / এটা **নয়** — he / it isn't\n\nIn relaxed Bangladeshi speech you'll hear plain **না** for everyone: *ota am na*. Learn both — the full set is used in writing and careful speech.\n\n**এটা / ওটা** = 'this / that'. The **-টা** on the end is a *classifier* — a little word Bengali attaches to things. You'll see lots of it in the Food unit.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "ki-q",
      "bn": "কি",
      "tr": "ki",
      "en": "(yes/no question marker)",
      "notes": "Turns a statement into a yes/no question: আপনি কি শিক্ষক? Usually comes right after the subject. Short ি — compare কী 'what'."
    },
    {
      "id": "noi",
      "bn": "নই",
      "tr": "noi",
      "en": "am not",
      "notes": "Goes with আমি / আমরা: আমি ছাত্র নই."
    },
    {
      "id": "noo",
      "bn": "নও",
      "tr": "noo",
      "en": "are not (familiar)",
      "notes": "Goes with তুমি / তোমরা."
    },
    {
      "id": "non",
      "bn": "নন",
      "tr": "non",
      "en": "is not / are not (polite)",
      "notes": "Goes with আপনি, তিনি, উনি."
    },
    {
      "id": "noy-isnt",
      "bn": "নয়",
      "tr": "noy",
      "en": "is not",
      "notes": "Goes with সে, ও, এটা. In everyday Bangladeshi speech না is used for all persons: আমি ছাত্র না. নয় is also the number nine (Unit 5)."
    },
    {
